  number={},
  pages={1-9},
  abstract={In recent times, there has been significant interest in the application of chaos-based metaheuristics in the literature and industry, although challenges remain to demonstrate consistent advantages over traditional approaches. In this work, four metaheuristics, namely the Genetic Algorithm (GA), Particle Swarm Optimisation (PSO), Hiking Optimisation Algorithm (HOA), and Komodo Milipir Algorithm (KMAD) are examined and compared to chaos-based variants of the four mentioned metaheuristics via 20 chaotic maps. To evaluate the performance of these metaheuristics, 12 widely used benchmark functions are employed with emphasis on metrics such as convergence times, diversity, and accuracy. Our findings have been obtained by extensive Monte Carlo numerical simulations. The results reveal that chaos-based metaheuristics converge faster and balance exploration and exploitation better, but show no notable accuracy improvement over standard versions.},
